"helpguide.title" = "How MFB Processes Your Media";
"helpguide.metadata.title" = "2. Metadata preservation";
"helpguide.metadata.intro" = "Preserving as much as possible is MFB's archival goal. It is not an unconditional guarantee for every mode, format or field. Check what the selected route retains and whether its required verification completed.";
"helpguide.metadata.1.title" = "Recovering JPEG bytes and displaying metadata differ";
"helpguide.metadata.1.body" = "Reversible JPEG → JXL requires the complete reconstructed JPEG to match the original byte for byte. This can retain vendor and opaque data inside the original JPEG through reconstruction. It does not mean every app can read or display that information directly from JXL. Independent display orientation, color and additional XMP require their own checks. Lossless pixels cannot replace these proofs.";
"helpguide.metadata.2.title" = "What is checked separately";
"helpguide.metadata.2.body" = "On preservation routes, MFB compares supported, portable EXIF, XMP, IPTC and other metadata, including readable capture times, descriptions and vendor fields. Separate checks cover ICC color profiles, orientation, pixels and container structure. Some structural fields change during conversion. A successful task alone cannot establish complete preservation of unrecognized, unrepresentable or unchecked information. For passthrough copies, matching file contents alone is also insufficient: reusing an existing file still requires checking the file attributes and XMP supported by that route.";
"helpguide.metadata.3.title" = "XMP companion files must not be selected arbitrarily";
"helpguide.metadata.3.body" = "XMP can store metadata in a separate file beside an image. MFB discovers supported companion names and checks the content needed for merging or delivery; a name alone does not prove historical ownership. Multiple candidates, an invalid file type or a read failure must be reported with the original retained. They must not be treated as no XMP or resolved by choosing an arbitrary candidate as preservation evidence.";
"helpguide.metadata.4.title" = "Fast AVIF deliberately clears metadata";
"helpguide.metadata.4.body" = "Fast AVIF's Meme Mode clears removable embedded metadata, including EXIF, XMP and IPTC, and verifies that clearing. This differs from ordinary JXL archival preservation. If your goal is to retain as much original media information as possible, do not treat Meme Mode as a complete-preservation mode. A higher quality value does not disable its metadata clearing.";
"helpguide.metadata.5.title" = "File attributes, Photos and iCloud have boundaries";
"helpguide.metadata.5.body" = "File times, permissions and supported extended attributes are preserved and checked separately, subject to filesystem precision and system behavior. Quarantine flags, compression implementation attributes and some dynamic system attributes are outside the byte-for-byte copy guarantee. Verified local Photos delivery does not establish that the library reads or displays every original field, or that iCloud has synced. Failed metadata, relationship or delivery evidence requires retaining affected originals; settings, thumbnails and matching counts cannot replace proof.";
"helpguide.tools.title" = "7. Retries, repair and tools";
"helpguide.tools.intro" = "Separate what recovery may do from which encoding tools may do it. Permission is not evidence that an operation ran or that a tool is available.";
"helpguide.tools.1.title" = "Encoding retries and repair: permitted operations";
"helpguide.tools.1.body" = "No extra recovery disables the compatibility retries and repairs governed by this setting. Fidelity-preserving retries may adjust parameters after supported JPEG failures, such as eligible E11-to-E10 retries, while still requiring exact original-JPEG reconstruction. Guarded repair additionally permits implemented recovery, such as patching a temporary color profile after a specific ICC D50 error. It does not promise to repair arbitrary damaged media or govern every internal retry.";
"helpguide.tools.2.title" = "Permitted encoding tools: who may perform them";
"helpguide.tools.2.body" = "Primary only forbids governed alternative encoding or recovery tools. Alternatives still require the route's other permissions. For example, an ImageMagick alternative in a D-search probe needs tool permission and expert mode; the recovery menu alone does not determine it. ffprobe detection, required decoding, ExifTool metadata work and djxl reconstruction checks are not all disabled by selecting the primary encoder only.";
"helpguide.tools.3.title" = "How the two choices work together";
"helpguide.tools.3.body" = "Alternatives allowed, extra recovery disabled: forbidden JPEG or ICC repairs remain forbidden. Guarded repair allowed, primary tool only: an eligible ICC patch may still retry the same cjxl, but does not authorize a second encoder. Full eleven-level JPEG comparison has a stricter boundary: it never changes candidate parameters or repairs input to fill a failed level. Any incomplete level leaves the comparison incomplete and retains the source. Applicability and tool availability must be confirmed at execution.";
"helpguide.tools.4.title" = "Batch continuation and final verification are separate";
"helpguide.tools.4.body" = "Record and continue is the default. Fast IMG exposes failure handling in Images settings; standard IMG/VID have separate Developer settings. Isolated file failures and Fast IMG pre-import verification failures are recorded and retain their sources. Other files still need all required checks before delivery. Untrusted shared import or delivery relationships still block delivery; not every Gate failure can be isolated. When stopping after a failure, Fast IMG starts no further group of tasks; tasks already running finish their current work and save checkpoints. Disabling compatibility retries does not mean fail-fast; switching tools or completing repair is not archival success. Required pixel, color, metadata, exact original-JPEG reconstruction and delivery checks remain mandatory. If safety cannot be proved, retain the source rather than relabeling failure as success or a skip.";
"helpguide.context" = "Choose a topic to see the processing rules, searches and safety checks.";
"helpguide.topics" = "Processing guide topics";
"helpguide.content" = "Processing guide content";
"helpguide.find" = "Find in this topic";
"helpguide.boundary" = "This guide explains the current private policy. A setting or preview is not proof that an individual file has passed verification.";
"helpguide.policy.title" = "1. Before Processing";
"helpguide.policy.intro" = "MFB is a private archival tool that aims to preserve media and metadata as completely as possible. Processing follows an established preservation policy; results depend on evidence for each file.";
"helpguide.policy.1.title" = "The policy comes before the settings";
"helpguide.policy.1.body" = "The policy decides which media may be converted, what must be retained and when an original may be removed. Settings adjust only the choices that policy permits. Choosing a quality value cannot unlock a forbidden conversion or disable required verification.";
"helpguide.policy.2.title" = "Inspect the content, not just the extension";
"helpguide.policy.2.body" = "MFB examines the actual media and relevant relationships. An image with a misleading extension is not renamed in the source folder just to make it recognizable. Preview and routing inspection explain selection; they do not encode, import or certify an archival result.";
"helpguide.policy.3.title" = "IMG handles still media; VID handles motion";
"helpguide.policy.3.body" = "A video container can enter IMG only after strict checks establish a single still frame and no audio, extra streams or timing meaning that must be preserved. Animation and video belong to the dynamic-media route. Uncertain or protected containers do not become ordinary images merely because one frame can be decoded.";
"helpguide.policy.4.title" = "Work folders and source-folder dates";
"helpguide.policy.4.body" = "MFB keeps formal processing state outside the source folder and checks matching recovery state before choosing a new output folder. It minimizes source-folder changes. It restores supported dates only when it can attribute the changes to its own work; concurrent changes and filesystem limits prevent an absolute zero-footprint guarantee.";
"helpguide.routes.title" = "3. What Happens to Each Image";
"helpguide.routes.intro" = "Different sources have different preservation contracts. One global D, E or quality value does not describe every route.";
"helpguide.routes.1.title" = "JPEG: preserve the original JPEG bytes";
"helpguide.routes.1.body" = "The reversible route stores JPEG data in JXL and must reconstruct the original JPEG byte for byte. This is not a new lossy pixel encode, and pixel distance D does not control it. JPEG Effort comparison is available in both ordinary IMG and Fast JXL, with separate saved choices for each.";
"helpguide.routes.2.title" = "Confirmed-lossless stills: keep lossless pixels";
"helpguide.routes.2.body" = "Genuine PNG and other admitted lossless stills use lossless JXL with D0. D0 is part of the preservation policy, not a removable default. Dimensions, color, bit depth and applicable metadata still need their own checks. A recognizable raster does not justify flattening a complex container.";
"helpguide.routes.3.title" = "Existing lossy media: do not blindly encode again";
"helpguide.routes.3.body" = "Eligible older lossy stills may use the existing guarded JXL quality search. Modern lossy formats and existing JXL normally retain their native encoding. The actual source, container rules and size policy decide admission; enabling search does not override those decisions.";
"helpguide.routes.4.title" = "Fast JXL and Fast AVIF are different workflows";
"helpguide.routes.4.body" = "Fast JXL centers on reversible JPEG conversion and its native-media delivery rules. Fast AVIF has its own Q/Speed search and first attempts true lossless where required. Its Meme Mode metadata-cleanup policy differs from ordinary JXL archival preservation. Existing AVIF is not re-encoded just because a Q value was selected.";
"helpguide.search.title" = "4. How the Searches Work";
"helpguide.search.intro" = "Automatic search compares real candidates inside the selected route. Fixed values are manual alternatives. These searches do not give permission to weaken the preservation policy.";
"helpguide.search.1.title" = "JPEG: compare every Effort and keep the smallest";
"helpguide.search.1.body" = "Full comparison covers E1 through E11. It tries E9 as an initial size reference, then completes all ten other efforts; low efforts are not skipped and E9 is not a predetermined winner. It compares complete JXL files after the same metadata handling, preferring lower E on equal sizes. The first usable candidate and every possible replacement must pass exact JPEG reconstruction. Larger non-contenders need no repeated reconstruction, and the winning file is reused. Unsupported or failed efforts still make the comparison incomplete and preserve the source. This order aims to reduce repeated checks, not guarantee faster processing for every image.";
"helpguide.search.2.title" = "JXL D: find the quality that meets the size rule";
"helpguide.search.2.body" = "D is distance: D0 means lossless; a larger permitted D allows more pixel change. Where the private policy permits exploration, the existing explorer measures a baseline, screens candidate distances, then searches and refines the useful range. Among candidates meeting the size rule it prefers lower D, then smaller size at equal D. Lossless-only sources stay D0.";
"helpguide.search.3.title" = "JXL E: compare encoding work at one quality";
"helpguide.search.3.body" = "Automatic pixel-E comparison tests a bounded set of Efforts at the same quality and selects a verified payload before D search continues in that selected E domain. It is a size comparison, not a promise of faster encoding or the best result across all E/D pairs. The selected Effort bounds this candidate set; fixed mode instead uses only that Effort.";
"helpguide.search.4.title" = "AVIF Q and Speed: locate, then confirm";
"helpguide.search.4.body" = "Higher AVIF Q means higher requested quality. Automatic search uses the existing bounded quality search; a saved fixed Q stays inactive until fixed mode is chosen. The locator uses Speed6, but its file is discarded before confirmation at the selected final Speed. The final domain is also used for verification. A failed fixed quality is not secretly lowered to create success.";
"helpguide.search.5.title" = "What automatic search does not mean";
"helpguide.search.5.body" = "E selection followed by D exploration is sequential, not an exhaustive joint search. It does not yet use a new low-E shortcut to prove a high-E result. JPEG byte reconstruction, lossless pixels and perceptual quality checks are different guarantees; none substitutes for metadata or delivery verification. Logs record actual candidates and failures for diagnosis.";
"helpguide.safety.title" = "5. Verification and Delivery";
"helpguide.safety.intro" = "Encoded does not mean verified. Verified locally does not mean imported into Photos. These stages have separate evidence and separate failures.";
"helpguide.safety.1.title" = "Validate the actual output";
"helpguide.safety.1.body" = "A successful encoder exit and a smaller file are only observations. MFB applies the route's required payload, image or reconstruction checks and binds the evidence to the current source and output. Invalid, changed or incomplete evidence blocks the relevant delivery or cleanup step.";
"helpguide.safety.2.title" = "Metadata is a separate obligation";
"helpguide.safety.2.body" = "Applicable XMP and sidecar relationships, color profiles, orientation, timestamps and other required media information are checked under the route's own contract. A filename match alone is not proof that a sidecar belongs to an image. Special HDR, gain-map or multi-image content cannot be silently discarded to make a conversion pass.";
"helpguide.safety.3.title" = "Photos import is an optional delivery step";
"helpguide.safety.3.body" = "When import is selected, MFB uses the configured Photos destination and backend, then checks delivery. Matching asset counts or a visible thumbnail alone do not prove the imported resource is correct. Photos or delivery failure is not reclassified as a harmless per-file conversion failure.";
"helpguide.safety.4.title" = "Delete only with the owning route's proof";
"helpguide.safety.4.body" = "Cleanup needs the owning workflow's completed verification and durable delivery evidence. Retained failures and intentionally skipped sources are not converted sources and must not be deleted as though they were. Unrecognized files in an output folder are not owned merely because the folder name ends in optimized.";
"helpguide.results.title" = "6. Results and Recovery";
"helpguide.results.intro" = "The summary stays short; the details and History preserve the evidence. A friendly message must not hide an incomplete or unsafe result.";
"helpguide.results.1.title" = "Understand the counts";
"helpguide.results.1.body" = "Processed files are divided into succeeded, failed, skipped and ignored. Discovered files also include anything not yet processed. A failed item is not a successful skip. Recovered or reused outputs are not counted as newly converted savings. Missing or inconsistent evidence must remain unknown, not become a guessed zero or MATCH.";
"helpguide.results.2.title" = "When a partial result can be amber";
"helpguide.results.2.body" = "A limited per-file failure may have an amber summary only when valid items completed the required checks, failed originals were verified as retained, counts reconcile and the required delivery/cleanup proof is complete. A gate failure, broken persistence, uncertain retention or batch-level error is a different result and is not softened into normal partial success.";
"helpguide.results.3.title" = "Restart from verified state, not a folder name";
"helpguide.results.3.body" = "Re-select the same input to let MFB inspect its matching checkpoint before allocating another output folder. Recovery still checks source identity and current evidence. An explicitly fresh run is different from resuming. Do not infer that an old optimized folder is complete or disposable just from its name.";
"helpguide.results.4.title" = "See what actually happened";
